Possible counts:

- 6: four lines in general position;
- 5: exactly two of the lines parallel;
- 4: two pairs of parallel lines;
- 3: three parallel lines and one line crossing them.

Why 2 is impossible — sort the lines into classes of parallel lines:

- all 4 parallel: 0 points; classes $3 + 1$: 3 points; classes $2 + 2$: 4 points;
- classes $2 + 1 + 1$: each of the two single lines crosses both parallel lines; they meet each other only once, so on at least one of the parallel lines they cross at different points, giving at least 3 points;
- no two parallel: if all four pass through one point there is 1 point; otherwise take two lines meeting at $P$ and a line not through $P$ — it meets them in two further points, so there are at least 3.

So exactly 2 intersection points never occur.

**Answer: (E) 2**
